1.Understanding bool Data Type in C#
-----------------------------------


What is bool?

The bool data type is used to store true or false values.

It comes from the word Boolean

It only allows two values:

1.true

2.false
-------------

 Real-Life Examples of Boolean Values
 
 | Real Life Situation            | Boolean (C#) Equivalent |
| ------------------------------ | ----------------------- |
| Is the light ON?               | `true` / `false`        |
| Do you have a driving license? | `true` / `false`        |
| Is user logged in?             | `true` / `false`        |
| Is payment completed?          | `true` / `false`        |

Syntax in C#
------------

bool isLoggedIn = true;
bool hasLicense = false;

 Example Program: Using bool

using System;

class BoolExample
{
    static void Main()
    {
        bool isStudent = true;
        bool hasPaidFees = false;

        Console.WriteLine("Is Student: " + isStudent);
        Console.WriteLine("Has Paid Fees: " + hasPaidFees);

        // Conditional check
        if (hasPaidFees)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Access Granted.");
        }
        else
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Access Denied. Please pay your fees.");
        }
    }
}

Explanation:-


isStudent = true → Student exists

hasPaidFees = false → Not paid yet

If condition checks hasPaidFees, and shows output accordingly.


Interview Tip:-
Q: “Can a bool store 1 or 0?”
A:  No. In C#, bool is strictly true or false. You cannot assign 1 or 0.

Summary for Notes:-
-----------------
| Keyword | Meaning      | Values       |
| ------- | ------------ | ------------ |
| `bool`  | Boolean type | `true/false` |

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



2.Understanding char Data Type in C#-

-What is char?
---------------
 
char stands for character, and it stores a single Unicode character.

It's always written inside single quotes: 'A', '1', '#'

It can store:

Alphabets → 'A', 'z'

Digits → '5' (as a character, not number)

Symbols → '%', '@'

Space → ' '
----------------------

Real-Life Examples of Characters:

| Example              | Stored As |
| -------------------- | --------- |
| First letter of name | `'V'`     |
| Grade                | `'A'`     |
| Keyboard input       | `'@'`     |
| Space                | `' '`     |

Syntax in C#
-----------

char grade = 'A';
char numberChar = '5';
char symbol = '$';
char space = ' ';

Important Rules:-
---------------
Use single quotes ' ', not double quotes

This is wrong: char ch = "A"; → Error! (double quotes = string)

Correct: char ch = 'A';


Example Program: Using char;-

using System;

class CharExample
{
    static void Main()
    {
        char initial = 'V';
        char grade = 'A';
        char symbol = '#';
        char space = ' ';

        Console.WriteLine("Initial: " + initial);
        Console.WriteLine("Grade: " + grade);
        Console.WriteLine("Symbol: " + symbol);
        Console.WriteLine("Space (watch carefully): [" + space + "]");
    }
}



Output:-
-------
 
Initial: V
Grade: A
Symbol: #
Space (watch carefully): [ ]


*Notice how even a space is a valid character and can be stored!


Summary for Notes:-

| Keyword | Description             | Example                    |
| ------- | ----------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `char`  | Stores 1 character only | `'A'`, `'%'`, `'5'`, `' '` |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



3.Understanding string Data Type in C#-
-----------------------------------


What is a string?

A string stores a sequence of characters — like words, names, or sentences.

It's written inside double quotes

It can include:

Letters: "Venkatesh"

Numbers: "123"

Symbols: "@abc#%"

Even spaces: "hello world"


Syntax-
------

string name = "Venkatesh";
string city = "Bangalore";
string mobile = "9876543210";

 Real-Life Examples of string-
 
 | Use Case            | Example             |
| ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Full Name           | `"Venkatesh"`       |
| Email ID            | `"venky@email.com"` |
| Address or Location | `"Bangalore"`       |
| Message to display  | `"Login failed"`    |


 Example Program-
 ---------------
 
 using System;
 class StringDatatypes
    {
    static void Main(String[] args)
    {
        string Name = "Venkatesh Reddy";
        string Email = "venkatesh@gmail.com";
        string Location = "Hyderabad";
        string DOB = "31/08/1999";
        string message = "login sucessuful";


        Console.WriteLine("Name of a Person :" + Name);
        Console.WriteLine("Email Id :"+Email);
        Console.WriteLine("Location :" + Location);
        Console.WriteLine("DOB :" + DOB);
        Console.WriteLine("Message :" + message);
    }
    }

	
Important Notes:-
----------------
 
| Rule                    | Example              |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------|
| Use double quotes `" "` | `"Hello"`            |
| `string` can be empty   | `string msg = "";`   |
| `string` can be `null`  | `string msg = null;` |


*String is a Reference Type, not a value type.
*It stores the address of the actual text in memory



Summary Table-

| Data Type | Meaning                       | Example          |
| --------- | ----------------------------- | ---------------- |
| `string`  | Sequence of characters (text) | `"Hello World!"` |


Completed:-
----------
bool – true/false

char – one character

string – full text/sentences
